# Abacus - the additive machine: each note is a row of up to 64 sine
# partials, every one with its own level and place in the pitch.
#
# The spectrum is a slope in dB per octave, odd against even partials, a peak,
# a vowel and a comb, and a second spectrum B, written as offsets from the
# first, that `morph` (and the morph envelope and LFO 1) moves to. `law` sets
# where the partials sit: harmonic, a stiff string, a free bar, a bell or a
# square plate. `damp` and `decay` make the high partials die first.
# `shimmer` drifts each partial's level and pitch on its own, `spread` places
# them across the stage.
#
# 96 patches in nine families, plainest first: classic (saw, square, pulse,
# triangle, then basses, leads and a pluck), organ (drawbar sets, pipes, reeds,
# a sub, a click, a driven organ, a theatre stop, a flute and a mixture), vocal
# (vowels, low and high voices, a hushed one, a walking vowel, a talking lead),
# strings (sections, a cello, a bass, plucked, trembling, a harp, a high sheet),
# mallets (bars, tines, a pan, a bass, a knock, ringing metal), bells (bell and
# plate laws, tubes, a comb, a plate, a bowl, chimes, a held cloud), count (the
# partials playing in rhythm, one pattern each, and a bass, plucks, a bell and
# a lead counted), borrow (the partials' levels from another track or a file)
# and slide (each partial arriving at the next note on its own time, or strummed
# in). Each patch names its phrase with `role=`; the bank's default is `pad`.
# Basses play `bass`, leads `lead`, two organs `keys`, plucks and mallets `tune`,
# bells `bell`, two count patches `arp` and the slide pads `slur`, a line of
# overlapping notes.
#
# Borrow: `borrow` 0 to 2 for off, track, file; `sidechain` the track (left
# at 0 here, the player picks it; the harness puts the patch's `input=` on
# track 1); `bmode` 0 envelope, 1 partials; `bamount` own to borrowed;
# `bsmooth` how fast the borrowed levels follow; `bfreeze` holds them; `bpos`
# and `bscan` where in the file and how fast it moves. The file patches name
# our sung line, `factory:voice` (files/borrow-voice.wav).
#
# Slide: `slide` 0.02 to 4 s is the longest a partial takes to reach the new
# note (the bottom is off); `sorder` 0 to 3 for low first, high first,
# scatter, centre out; `sspread` how far apart the times are; `slegato` 0
# always, 1 legato only (a key still held or just let go); `strum` 0 to 1 s
# is how long the partials take to come in at a note's start.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Abacus` lists
# them. Every patch but Init names every parameter. `partials` is 0, 1 or 2
# for 16, 32 or 64; `law` 0 to 4 for harmonic, string, bar, bell, plate;
# `spreadmode` 0 to 2 for odd/even, scatter, low-high; `count` 0 to 6 for off, up,
# down, up-down, odd/even, fifths, random; `crate` 0 to 9 for 1/32T, 1/32, 1/16T,
# 1/16, 1/8T, 1/8, 1/4T, 1/4, 1/2, bar (from the song's tick, swing followed); the LFO waves are
# sine, tri, saw up, saw down, square, s&h, smooth, 8 step, 16 step.
#
# Levels: everything but the struck patches sits at -27.5 on `loud`, the gated
# level over its own phrase, within 1 dB, Init included; that takes in the
# plucks in classic and strings, Bell Haze and Bell Count. Mallets, bells and
# the struck slide and borrow patches sit at -25.8 on the loudest 400 ms (the
# rows ending in `m`). Init gets there through the machine's house trim, with
# `volume` at its default of 0.18, 15 dB under the top. The patches are levelled
# by `volume`, never higher than 0.5, 6 dB under the top; a patch that needs
# more takes the rest from the gain of its last insert, or from an Eq insert
# that only sets its gain. Rock Organ keeps its volume, since it sets the
# drive, and is levelled by the Distortion's gain.
#
# Every patch but Init carries eight named macros. Destinations are numbered
# as in Abacus.h's MacroDest: #1 slope, #2 odd, #3 peak, #4 peakgain, #5
# peakwidth, #6 vowel, #7 vowelamt, #8 comb, #9 stretch, #10 bslope, #11 bodd,
# #12 bpeak, #13 bcomb, #14 morph, #15 menv, #16 mtime, #17 damp, #18 decay,
# #19 attack, #20 decay2, #21 sustain, #22 release, #23 velocity, #24
# velbright, #25 spread, #26 shimmer, #27 shrate, #28 lfo1rate, #29 lfo1pitch,
# #30 lfo1morph, #31 lfo2rate, #32 lfo2bright, #33 lfo2pan, #34 glide, #35
# fine, #36 mpetimbre, #37 mpepressure, #38 volume, #39 pan, #40 pitch (24
# semitones a unit), #41 cdepth, #42 cglide, #43 bamount, #44 bsmooth, #45
# bpos, #46 bscan, #47 slide, #48 sspread, #49 strum.
#
# Each family has its own eight. Classic and strings: Bright, Hollow, Morph,
# Fade, Shimmer, Wide, Slow and Comb (strings end on Stiff). Organ swaps Fade
# for Click and Slow for Vibrato. Vocal has Bright, Vowel, Morph, Voice,
# Shimmer, Wide, Slow and Peak. Mallets have Bright, Strike, Morph, Ring,
# Damp, Wide, Shimmer and Bar. Bells have Bright, Morph, Ring, Damp, Stretch,
# Shimmer, Wide and Swell. Count has Bright, Depth, Glide (the count's
# crossfade), Morph, Shimmer, Wide, Slow and Comb. Borrow has Amount, Smooth,
# then Hold, Peak, Vowel or (from a file) Place and Scan, Bright, Morph,
# Shimmer, Wide and Slow. Slide has Bright, Slide, Spread, Strum, then Morph
# (Vowel on two), Shimmer, Wide and Slow; the two struck ones end Morph, Ring,
# Wide and Damp. A lead swaps the family's last macro for Glide, the pitch
# glide (Quick Run calls the count's crossfade Blur), and a bass has Release in
# place of Slow. Fade and Damp need a patch whose partials decay; on a held
# one Fade shortens the note's partials as well. Where a macro moves the level
# by more than a decibel it also has a route onto `volume` (#38) that keeps
# the patch near the house level at full turn.

machine Abacus
role    pad



# --- classic ------------------------------------------------------------------

patch Init family=classic note=60 range=36..84

patch "Saw Stack" family=classic note=60 range=36..84
  # The plain saw with a slow shimmer and a little width.
  set macro1 "Bright"
  set macro2 "Hollow"
  set macro3 "Morph"
  set macro4 "Fade"
  set macro5 "Shimmer"
